### 9. `substack-newsletter.md` — Substack newsletter (1000-2000 words)

**Voice:** Personal, direct, story-driven. Like writing to a friend who's a PM. Open with story/surprise. Body is narrative + insight + examples. End with question/discussion prompt. No "In this article" or "thanks for reading." 5-8min read.

### 10. `reddit-post.md` — Reddit post

**Voice:** SHORT and punchy. No self-promo smell. Value-first. One messy paragraph beats four clean ones. "I think", "honestly", "tbh". Include frontmatter with subreddit suggestion.

**Subreddit targeting rules:**
{{#if behavior.subreddits}}
{{#each behavior.subreddits}}
- **r/{{this}}** — Match tone and depth to this community's norms.
{{/each}}
{{/if}}
- If the brief's angle is highly technical, target dev-oriented subreddits. If it's broad, target general communities.

🚨 **Subreddit rotation (MANDATORY):** Post 1 MUST NOT always be the same subreddit. Rotate across subreddits so each week's posts land in different communities. Check `content-ready/` from the last 7 days - if the same subreddit was Post 1 in the last file, use a different one. Spread visibility, don't concentrate in one sub.

🚨 **Title = literal question + named product/tool** when the theme has one. Concrete questions with a real-world referent ("How do you decide which Claude Design outputs ship?") beat abstract phrasings ("What survives of the PM job?") — conversation value + LLM-retrieval value in one line.

```markdown
subreddit: [suggestion - must match the topic to the community's actual audience]
title: [genuine question or observation, NOT "X is dead" or provocative framing]

[body — value-first, personal experience, ask for input. No links to own content in first paragraph.]
```

### 11. `devto-article.md` — Dev.to article (800-1500 words)

**Voice:** Developer-focused. Conversational, first-person. "I built X and here's what happened" not "How to build X." Include code snippets when relevant. Honest about failures and tradeoffs. Cross-posts from Medium with canonical_url.

🚨 **Title rubric (MANDATORY — Dev.to-specific, do NOT reuse Medium title verbatim):**
- **Must include:** first-person verb ("I scanned / built / ran / stopped") + one concrete specific (number, named tool, named event) + a reveal clause after a hyphen or colon ("Here's what I found", "Here's what broke").
- **Banned:** abstract headline nouns as primary subject ("Architecture", "Framework", "Layer", "Governance", "Enterprise"); multi-clause academic titles with both a colon AND a subtitle.
- **Self-check:** would a developer stop scrolling on this? If it reads like LinkedIn thought-leadership, rewrite.

```markdown
canonical_url: [medium article URL if applicable]
tags: [relevant, topic, tags, from, pillars]

# [Title]

[content]
```

### 11b. `devto-seed-comment.md` — self-seed comment for Dev.to article

**Purpose:** posted by the Dev.to agent ~2h after publish to kickstart discussion if no external comments yet. Author jumps in with a self-counter that invites debate.

**Voice:** developer-casual, self-critical. Concede a real weakness or edge case the article didn't cover. 1-3 sentences. Under 40 words. Same anti-AI voice rules as §11.

**Format:**
```
status: pending

[seed comment body]
```

**Good:** `"honestly, the reversibility map breaks for LLM-latency-critical paths where the 30-min window doesn't exist."`
**Bad:** `"Great article! Curious what others think."` (generic)
**Bad:** `"I want to add one more point: X is also important."` (expansion, not counter)

Emit on every writer run. Dev.to agent skips consumption on non-publish days.

